driving today might feel like you're  using a tech gadget  especially if it's a car developed by  chinese automaker x-pung  first you'll need your phone to activate  the features and you need to  scan it then you'll give commands  and when you get to your destination the  vehicle will perk itself  these features are all part of x-punk's  strategy to stand out in the world's  second biggest market for electric  vehicles  the company is competing in china not  only with ev leaders like tesla  all-electric f-150 lightning but also  traditional automakers like ford and gm  which are doubling down on producing  electric vehicles too  technology combined with mobility and  auto  is where we think the future is going to  be one high-tech feature automakers are  racing to build  full self-driving capabilities but  putting more tech in vehicles creates  engineering challenges  and also risky behaviors it's totally  asleep  like tesla x-punk is trying to push the  boundaries of integrating tech into our  cars  and has gotten into a spat with elon  musk over software so we went to  x-pung's r d  lab to understand its vision for driving  we are working out a very unique culture  and capability that allows to  rapidly innovate will be able to be one  of the top players in china  has always operated like a tech startup  since it was founded in 2014.  we have a lot of the traditional auto  background employees  at the same time we have staff coming  from leading technology companies  brian goo is a former j.p morgan  executive who took over as  x-pung's vice chairman and president in  2018. we actually dedicate  close to 40 of our staff in the r d  area in software so i think that's very  different compared to traditional auto  companies  goo was the man behind x-punk's ipo on  the new york stock exchange last year  that raised 1.7 billion dollars  using this money expunge hired more  engineers and poached talent from  china's top tech companies like tencent  and alibaba  and one of the big goals for this larger  engineering team is to develop  self-driving technology  currently expunge offers what it calls  advanced driver assistance systems  this controls the car on a highway but  you have to keep your hands on the  steering wheel  in five years x-punk's goal is to  develop a fully autonomous vehicle that  doesn't need anyone in the seat  to make this happen it's invested a lot  in software and hardware  we are keeping basically investment in  autonomous driving as our  number one priority shinzo wu leads  expungs autonomous driving division  its software decides when the car should  accelerate or break and that relies on  information that comes from a vast array  of sensors  plus the most recent exponent cars are  going to be equipped with a special  piece of hardware called lidar which  uses light waves to map what's around  lilac can do a range calculation to get  a kind of a 3d  world view we can make a ton of driving  the city safer  lidar is expensive typically costing  about a thousand dollars apiece  and x-pung's latest car will take two  the company will be the first automaker  to mass-produce lighter equipped  vehicles this year  and its entry point will be lower than  35 thousand dollars  but x-punk's tech strategy has some big  detractors  is a fool's errand expensive sensors  that are unnecessary tesla is taking a  different approach and instead  investing in software that can interpret  what's on the road using only regular  cameras  tesla has very much tried to do  self-driving on the cheap  um with the the bare minimum number of  sensors that they can that they think  they can get away with  sam abul sammit is an auto analyst who's  been analyzing ev tech features for the  past 10 years  during a recent road test of ev  self-driving capabilities  tesla came in first by a big margin but  able simon says  x-pung system has more potential because  of its hardware  with the right software you can do  distance measurement but it's never  going to be as accurate or as reliable  as what you could do with active sensors  like lidar  a lot of sensors and cameras potentially  mean the self-driving software is  receiving more useful information about  what's on the road  just compare xpung's 32 sensors in its  latest sedan  to the 12 sensors and 8 cameras in a  tesla vehicle  tesla didn't respond to a request for  comment but musk has tweeted several  times  that its camera based system is more  precise than sensors like lidar  but the tesla and x-punk rivalry goes  beyond choice of hardware  it all came to a head when tesla filed a  lawsuit in 2019 against one of its  former employees in the u.s  who had quit to join x-punk the lawsuit  alleged that he took copies of the  source code of tesla's current driver  assistance system  in order to hand it over to his new  employer in court documents tesla  alleged the chinese company was inspired  by  and on a mission to beat tesla and has  transparently imitated tesla's design  technology and even its business model  in april tessa settled the lawsuit with  the former employee who acknowledged he  shouldn't have saved the source code  but said he didn't provide it to expunge  xbox said the engineer no longer works  for the company  and that its in-house developed r d has  been its competitive advantage  i view tesla as our partner rather than  competitor  tesla never brought that claim against  our company  which means that there is no evidence to  point any  wrongdoing but if there's one challenge  that the two rivals are facing together  it's the uphill battle with the public  perception of their prized  tech there's a growing sense that  the driver assistance systems that they  have the capabilities have been oversold  edward nedermeyer is an industry  observer who pushes for self-driving  technology as a way to make roads safer  okay tesla let's get this trip started  but he says social media video showing  some drivers operating a tesla from the  back seat  shows how dangerous these systems can be  when people are not using them properly  there's considerable  research that shows us people are much  more likely to misuse those systems  if they overestimate their capability  tesla has repeatedly said the features  it offers make driving safer  meanwhile expunge is working on a  driver's test acknowledging that a  self-driving system has limitations  for our drivers to be able to unlock the  autonomous driving features  they actually need to take a test we  also need to educate the drivers  how to properly use those technologies  so while expunge hones its self-driving  capabilities engineers are beefing up  other tech features like its voice  recognition system  or making its battery management  software super efficient  xbone has already started selling cars  in norway and says these tweaks will  help it crack the larger europe market  and someday the company plans to have  its cars on u.s roads  right alongside tesla vehicles
 

